<?xml version="1.0" ?>
<!--
  @DOCK_MODEL@: dock @DOCK_LETTER@, carrying tag36h11 id @DOCK_TAG_ID@.

  GENERATED FILE - do not edit. The source is dock_template/model.sdf.in, and
  CMakeLists.txt configures it once per dock so that all eight docks share one
  set of numbers. The eight differ only in the two identity textures and the
  name.

  This is the delivery target the rover drives into.

  Built to DOCK_PLAN.md, which is the single source of truth for the geometry.
  All numbers there are millimetres; SDF is metres, so every dimension below is
  that value divided by 1000.

  Shape is a shallow trapezoidal bay - a flat back wall carrying the AprilTag
  plus two wings flared out at exactly 45 deg. A flat wall would leave position
  ALONG the wall unobservable to a 2D LiDAR, so a scan match against it slides
  sideways; three segments meeting at two known angles constrain x, y and yaw.
  The 45 deg flare is what makes the dock distinguishable from a straight wall
  (0 deg) and a box corner (90 deg), so do not round it off. The wings are a
  perception feature and a soft limit, NOT a funnel: a skid-steer drivetrain has
  very high lateral friction, so a robot that touches a wing pushes and stalls
  rather than sliding into alignment.

  Frame: origin at floor level on the centre of the back wall's inner face.
  +X out of the dock into the arena (the approach direction), +Y left as seen
  from the dock looking outward, +Z up. The bay occupies positive X, so a robot
  approaching the dock travels in -X.

  All panels are 8 mm thick boxes rather than meshes: no external asset to keep
  in sync, and the small mitre overlap where a wing meets the back wall is
  harmless. Every panel is solid from the floor up - a panel on legs would let
  the LiDAR scan plane pass under it and see two thin posts instead of a wall.

  ==========================================================================
  WHAT THE APPEARANCE IS ALLOWED TO TOUCH

  Everything the robot senses lives in a narrow slice of this model, so the
  styling below is confined to the parts that are provably outside it:

    inner faces, z  80..180  FROZEN. Planar, continuous, light, matte. This is
                             the mandated legal scan-height band and the whole
                             of the LiDAR contract.
    tag plate + margin       FROZEN. y +-62.5, z 39.5..164.5, and nothing
                             decorative below z=200, which leaves 20 mm of
                             light wall above the band and 35.5 mm above the
                             plate.
    inward protrusions
    below z=180              FORBIDDEN. Occlusion or shadow on the tag, and
                             DOCK_PLAN.md 7.2 already bans anything standing in
                             the bay footprint.
    inner faces, z 200..350  Free - texture and geometry. No legal scan height
                             reaches it.
    above z=350, and the
    outer faces              Free. The robot never sees these from the bay.

  So: the accent band, the crown, the pylon and the stepped wing tops are all at
  z >= 200. Nothing below that moved. Two side effects are improvements rather
  than costs - the panels went from 0.82 to 0.90 diffuse, which is MORE
  reflective in the band, not less; and the added pattern gives the OAK-D's
  stereo pair something to correlate on, where a blank wall is close to its
  worst case.

  The one rule that holds everywhere: keep it matte. Gloss anywhere near the tag
  specular-blows-out under arena lights.
-->
<sdf version="1.10">
  <model name="@DOCK_MODEL@">
    <static>true</static>

    <link name="dock_body">

      <!-- ============================ walls ============================ -->

      <!-- Back wall: 8 x 400 x 350, inner face at x=0, spans y -200..+200.
           Deliberately left as one monolithic panel. DOCK_PLAN.md 7.3 holds it
           to +-1.0 mm flatness over its 400 mm because that error lands
           directly in the tag pose, and a horizontal joint here would be the
           easiest way to lose that. The accent band is applied over it rather
           than built into it for the same reason. -->
      <visual name="back_wall_visual">
        <pose>-0.004 0 0.175 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.008 0.400 0.350</size></box></geometry>
        <!-- Off-white and matte: ~90 % reflectivity gives the LiDAR full range,
             where a dark or glossy dock returns weakly. -->
        <material>
          <ambient>0.62 0.62 0.62 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.90 0.90 0.89 1</diffuse>
          <specular>0.04 0.04 0.04 1</specular>
        </material>
      </visual>
      <collision name="back_wall_collision">
        <pose>-0.004 0 0.175 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.008 0.400 0.350</size></box></geometry>
      </collision>

      <!-- Wings, each split into two coplanar 120 mm sections so the tops can
           step. The split falls at the 120 mm mark, which is exactly where
           DOCK_PLAN.md 7.2 already seams the wing for printing - so this costs
           no extra parts, only a shorter outer one.

           Both sections share one inner-face plane and abut exactly at the
           seam, so the LiDAR still sees one unbroken line. Full wing inner face
           runs (0, 200) -> (169.71, 369.71); local +X points at the mouth, and
           each centre is its section's inner-face midpoint offset 4 mm along
           the panel's outward normal.

           Inner sections stay 350 tall. Outer sections drop to 260, which is
           what gives the dock its stepped skyline; 260 is still 80 mm clear
           above the 180 mm top of the mandated band, so the wall the LiDAR
           needs is untouched. -->
      <visual name="left_wing_inner_visual">
        <pose>0.03960 0.24525 0.175 0 0 0.785398</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.120 0.008 0.350</size></box></geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.62 0.62 0.62 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.90 0.90 0.89 1</diffuse>
          <specular>0.04 0.04 0.04 1</specular>
        </material>
      </visual>
      <collision name="left_wing_inner_collision">
        <pose>0.03960 0.24525 0.175 0 0 0.785398</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.120 0.008 0.350</size></box></geometry>
      </collision>

      <visual name="left_wing_outer_visual">
        <pose>0.12445 0.33011 0.130 0 0 0.785398</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.120 0.008 0.260</size></box></geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.62 0.62 0.62 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.90 0.90 0.89 1</diffuse>
          <specular>0.04 0.04 0.04 1</specular>
        </material>
      </visual>
      <collision name="left_wing_outer_collision">
        <pose>0.12445 0.33011 0.130 0 0 0.785398</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.120 0.008 0.260</size></box></geometry>
      </collision>

      <!-- Right wing: the left wing mirrored in Y. -->
      <visual name="right_wing_inner_visual">
        <pose>0.03960 -0.24525 0.175 0 0 -0.785398</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.120 0.008 0.350</size></box></geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.62 0.62 0.62 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.90 0.90 0.89 1</diffuse>
          <specular>0.04 0.04 0.04 1</specular>
        </material>
      </visual>
      <collision name="right_wing_inner_collision">
        <pose>0.03960 -0.24525 0.175 0 0 -0.785398</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.120 0.008 0.350</size></box></geometry>
      </collision>

      <visual name="right_wing_outer_visual">
        <pose>0.12445 -0.33011 0.130 0 0 -0.785398</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.120 0.008 0.260</size></box></geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.62 0.62 0.62 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.90 0.90 0.89 1</diffuse>
          <specular>0.04 0.04 0.04 1</specular>
        </material>
      </visual>
      <collision name="right_wing_outer_collision">
        <pose>0.12445 -0.33011 0.130 0 0 -0.785398</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.120 0.008 0.260</size></box></geometry>
      </collision>

      <!-- ======================= crown and pylon ======================= -->

      <!-- Crown: 8 x 400 x 60 capping the back wall, top edge at z=410. It
           carries the wordmark as its own surface texture rather than on a
           proud plate - authored at the 400:60 face aspect, so the dark field
           around the mark IS the panel and there is no seam to match or
           z-fight.

           NOTE the wordmark is a placeholder set in Nimbus Sans, not the real
           logotype. Drop the brand asset in over materials/textures/wordmark.png
           at the same 400:60 aspect and nothing else needs to change. -->
      <visual name="crown_visual">
        <pose>-0.004 0 0.380 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.008 0.400 0.060</size></box></geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>1 1 1 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>1 1 1 1</diffuse>
          <specular>0 0 0 1</specular>
          <pbr>
            <metal>
              <albedo_map>model://@DOCK_MODEL@/materials/textures/wordmark.png</albedo_map>
              <metalness>0</metalness>
              <roughness>0.9</roughness>
            </metal>
          </pbr>
        </material>
      </visual>
      <collision name="crown_collision">
        <pose>-0.004 0 0.380 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.008 0.400 0.060</size></box></geometry>
      </collision>

      <!-- Pylon: 8 x 160 x 120 centred on the crown, top edge at z=530. Carries
           the dock identity letter, again as its own face texture at the 160:120
           aspect. This is dock @DOCK_LETTER@, and the letter always matches
           the tag id, so the human-readable marking and the machine-readable
           one cannot drift apart.

           This is also where DOCK_PLAN.md 4.2's optional second tag goes if
           discovery is ever needed: a 150 mm tag centred at z=470 replaces the
           letter and needs no new structure, where the original variant had to
           raise the whole back wall to 500 mm to find the room. -->
      <visual name="pylon_visual">
        <pose>-0.004 0 0.470 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.008 0.160 0.120</size></box></geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>1 1 1 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>1 1 1 1</diffuse>
          <specular>0 0 0 1</specular>
          <pbr>
            <metal>
              <albedo_map>model://@DOCK_MODEL@/materials/textures/@DOCK_LETTER_TEXTURE@</albedo_map>
              <metalness>0</metalness>
              <roughness>0.9</roughness>
            </metal>
          </pbr>
        </material>
      </visual>
      <collision name="pylon_collision">
        <pose>-0.004 0 0.470 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.008 0.160 0.120</size></box></geometry>
      </collision>

      <!-- ========================= accent band ========================= -->

      <!-- A dark band wrapping all three inner faces at z 200..260, 1 mm proud
           of the wall the way applied vinyl would be. This is the one styling
           element on the surface the robot actually looks at, and it is what
           keeps the wall from being a blank field; it also ties the outer wings'
           new 260 mm top edge into the composition, since that edge is exactly
           the top of the band.

           z 200..260 is chosen, not convenient: 20 mm above the 180 mm ceiling
           of the mandated LiDAR band, and 35.5 mm above the tag plate's top
           edge at 164.5. No collision - it is paint, not structure.

           One overlay per wing covers both of that wing's sections, because the
           two are coplanar. -->
      <visual name="back_wall_band_visual">
        <pose>0.0005 0 0.230 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.001 0.400 0.060</size></box></geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.050 0.050 0.055 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.085 0.085 0.095 1</diffuse>
          <specular>0.02 0.02 0.02 1</specular>
        </material>
      </visual>

      <visual name="left_wing_band_visual">
        <pose>0.08520 0.28450 0.230 0 0 0.785398</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.240 0.001 0.060</size></box></geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.050 0.050 0.055 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.085 0.085 0.095 1</diffuse>
          <specular>0.02 0.02 0.02 1</specular>
        </material>
      </visual>

      <visual name="right_wing_band_visual">
        <pose>0.08520 -0.28450 0.230 0 0 -0.785398</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.240 0.001 0.060</size></box></geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.050 0.050 0.055 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.085 0.085 0.095 1</diffuse>
          <specular>0.02 0.02 0.02 1</specular>
        </material>
      </visual>

      <!-- ============================= tag ============================= -->

      <!-- AprilTag tag36h11 id 0, centred at z=102 mm - on the OAK-D Lite's
           optical axis. DOCK_PLAN.md asks for "a base-mounted camera height"
           and assumed 200 mm; the rover's camera is actually at z=101.953 mm
           (oak_d_lite_link on base_footprint, which rests at z~0), so 102 mm
           is that intent with the measured number. A tag mounted high would
           force a camera mast, which then punches a hole in the robot's own
           LiDAR scan plane.

           Centring on the axis rather than above it is what makes the tag
           usable at close range. The vertical half-FOV is 27.0 deg
           (atan(390/765.33)), so the standoff at which the 100 mm black
           square still fits the frame is set by how far the tag's furthest
           edge sits off the optical axis:

             tag centre z    black square in frame from   whole plate from
                200 mm              231 mm standoff           256 mm
                140 mm              114 mm                    138 mm
                102 mm               39 mm                     64 mm

           At 200 mm the tag was invisible over almost all of the 150-250 mm
           scoring window. On the axis it clears the window by a factor of
           four, and pitch and ride-height changes now move the tag towards
           the middle of the frame instead of out of the top of it.
           Horizontal never binds: 34.5 deg half-FOV takes the 125 mm plate
           down to a 32 mm standoff.

           Measured against that prediction, in 2 mm steps: detection starts
           at a 39 +/- 1 mm standoff (38 fails, 40 detects at margin 72) and
           holds unbroken out to 3 m, the far end of the sweep. At the 200 mm
           docked pose the tag lands within 0.1 mm of the optical axis, its
           black square measures 100.0 mm, and the recovered range is 0.6 mm
           long. Range bias grows with distance as it always does on a 100 mm
           tag - +11 mm at 1 m, +97 mm at 3 m - so trust the tag for bearing
           at range and for range only up close.

           The plate is 125 mm square, NOT 100 mm. The source PNG is 10 x 10
           cells - an 8 x 8 tag inside a 1-cell white margin - so the black
           border square covers 8/10 of the plate: 125 * 8/10 = 100 mm, which is
           the figure apriltag_ros wants in `size` (size: 0.100). Sizing the
           plate to 100 mm would give an 80 mm black square and put every
           downstream pose estimate 20 % out.

           The texture is upscaled from 10 x 10 to 1000 x 1000 with
           nearest-neighbour only; any smooth filter blurs the cell edges and
           the detector stops seeing it.

           1 mm thick and centred at x=0.0005, so the visible face sits 1 mm
           proud of the wall's inner face and cannot z-fight with it. A detector
           therefore reports the tag plane at x=1 mm, not x=0. No collision
           geometry - the tag is paint, not structure.

           The plate now spans z 39.5..164.5 mm, so it crosses the 80-180 mm
           LiDAR band that the wall height is chosen to cover - on this rover
           the scan plane at z=145 mm passes over the tag. That is a print
           consideration, not a modelling one: gz-sim's gpu_lidar ignores
           material, and the plate is only 1 mm proud, well under the LD19P's
           15 mm range noise (measured: it leaves the back-wall segment fit
           unchanged). On the physical dock, matte black paper in the scan band
           returns more weakly than the surrounding light panel. The 200 mm
           baseline overlapped the same band at 137.5..180 mm, so this is not a
           new problem, and no tag height avoids it - clearing the band
           entirely would need the plate below z=17.5 mm, i.e. underground.

           This dock carries id @DOCK_TAG_ID@. See the dock table in
           DOCK_PLAN.md 3.4 for the full letter/id/pose assignment. -->
      <visual name="apriltag_visual">
        <pose>0.0005 0 0.102 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.001 0.125 0.125</size></box></geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>1 1 1 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>1 1 1 1</diffuse>
          <specular>0 0 0 1</specular>
          <pbr>
            <metal>
              <albedo_map>model://@DOCK_MODEL@/materials/textures/@DOCK_TAG_TEXTURE@</albedo_map>
              <metalness>0</metalness>
              <roughness>0.9</roughness>
            </metal>
          </pbr>
        </material>
      </visual>

    </link>

    <!-- Pose of a correctly docked robot, in the dock frame. Delivery is
         defined as arrival here; nothing is physically transferred.

         This is the pose of the rover's MODEL ORIGIN, which is base_footprint -
         the frame DiffDrive publishes odom against - not base_link.

         Derived from einride_mini_truck_description rather than assumed. The
         rover's front-most point is the front wheel outer edge: wheel link at
         x=+0.08596 with a mesh reaching +0.040, so +0.12596 ahead of
         base_footprint (the chassis mesh itself stops at +0.12306, and
         base_link sits 0.46 mm ahead of base_footprint). Overall length is
         0.2510 m and base_footprint is centred in it to within 0.5 mm.
         Front face 200 mm off the back wall therefore puts base_footprint at
         0.200 + 0.12596 = 0.32596, i.e. 0.326 to the nearest millimetre.

         Yaw 180 deg: the robot's +X points at the back wall.

         Scoring tolerance is standoff 150-250 mm, yaw error < 6 deg,
         stationary for 2 s - so the 200 mm nominal has 50 mm either way. -->
    <frame name="dock_approach_pose">
      <pose>0.326 0 0 0 0 3.14159265</pose>
    </frame>

  </model>
</sdf>
